WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.159
Numerical error has two different sources

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:06.838
Conditioning describes sensitivity built into the mathematical problem

00:00:06.838 --> 00:00:18.333
Near input one, the reciprocal of x minus one changes sharply: moving from one point zero one to one point zero two halves the output.

00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:23.961
Algorithmic stability concerns additional error introduced by the computation

00:00:23.961 --> 00:00:28.288
Subtracting nearly equal rounded values can erase meaningful digits

00:00:28.288 --> 00:00:38.867
If this square root is first rounded to two decimal places, the subtraction becomes zero even though the true difference is positive.

00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:48.139
Multiplying by the conjugate gives an equivalent quotient with numerator one and denominator about two hundred

00:00:48.139 --> 00:00:52.125
This avoids subtracting two nearly equal intermediate values

00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:59.100
The rewrite changes the computational route while preserving the exact mathematical quantity being requested.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.807
Relative error compares the absolute error with the size of a nonzero true answer

00:01:06.807 --> 00:01:12.299
A tiny absolute error can be enormous relative to a tiny result

00:01:12.299 --> 00:01:17.333
Improving an algorithm cannot remove sensitivity intrinsic to uncertain input data.
